WEBVTT
TITLE Proving a Language Is Not Context-Free: the Pumping Lemma as a Game, Ogden’s Lemma and the Closure Route — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
The pumping lemma is a one-way test: it can prove a language is not context-free, and it can never prove one is.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Every context-free language has a number p: every string of length p or more splits into five parts.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The two middle parts may be repeated together: v to the i, x, y to the i is still in the language for every i.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
The GATE 2027 syllabus names it directly in Section 6: regular and context-free languages, pumping lemma.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
A long string has a tall parse tree, and a tree taller than the number of nonterminals repeats one on a path.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The repeated pair cuts the string into five pieces: the lower copy of the nonterminal derives v x y.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Copying the upper subtree in place of the lower one repeats v and y together, so every i is still derivable.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The pumping length is the grammar’s own number: p = 2 to the k for a grammar in Chomsky normal form with k nonterminals.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Condition one: the window v x y is short, at most p letters. Condition two: v and y are not both empty.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The short window is what makes the lemma bite: a split may only disturb a bounded piece of the string.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The quantifier order is the whole lemma: there exists p, for every long enough w, there exists a split, for every i.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Read it as a game: you pick w, the adversary picks the split, you pick i, and you win if some i leaves the language.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Take w as p a’s, then p b’s, then p c’s. Its length is 3p, so the lemma applies to it.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The window has at most p letters and the middle block has exactly p b’s, so it cannot touch all three blocks.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Case one: the window sits inside one block, so pumping changes one count and the three counts no longer agree.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Case two: the window straddles two blocks, so two counts move and the third stays, which is again a contradiction.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Run the lemma as a search: fix p, then try every allowed split of every string in a stated family.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
On aⁿbⁿcⁿ the search refutes p = 1 through 6, each time finding a string with no surviving split.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The witnesses are computed, not chosen: abc refutes p = 1, 2 and 3, and aabbcc refutes p = 4, 5 and 6.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
A refutation at one p is not a proof: the lemma needs some p to work, so a finite search can never finish the job.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
aⁿbⁿ is context-free, and the search refutes it at p = 1: the two-letter string ab has no surviving split.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
At p = 2 it survives, and at every p up to 6: take one a as v and one b as y, and both counts move together.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
So a failed pumping length is not a counterexample to the lemma. It says only that p was too small.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The lemma is necessary and never sufficient, and the next two scenes are the two ways that bites.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Ogden’s lemma lets you mark letters first: the pump must touch a marked letter and the window may carry at most p marked ones.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Marking turns a weak condition into a strong one: you decide where the adversary is allowed to pump.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
On aⁿbⁿcⁿ, marking the a’s cuts the cases: the search tries 2, 13, 44, 110, 230 and 427 splits at p = 1 to 6.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Same language, same conclusion, fewer cases: a split that touches a b or a c is killed by the marking at once.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Take the strings a to the i, b to the j, c to the k, d to the l where i is 0 or j equals k equals l.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
The plain lemma survives at every p from 1 to 6, over 228 tested strings: when there is no a, pump any b.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Ogden’s lemma refutes it at every p: marking the b’s forces the pump into a block whose count must stay equal.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
And it is not context-free: intersecting it with a-plus b-star c-star d-star leaves three equal counts, which fail at every p.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Most exam questions fall to closure instead of the lemma: intersect with a regular set, or apply a homomorphism.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
GATE CSE 2019 asked which of four languages is not context-free. Computed: w aⁿ w-reverse bⁿ is refuted at every p.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Its neighbours survive: w w-reverse and w aⁿ bⁿ w-reverse from p = 2, and aⁿ b to the i with i in n, 3n, 5n at p = 6.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
GATE CSE 2016 Set 2 paired aⁿbᵐcⁿ⁺ᵐ, which survives from p = 2, with aⁿbⁿc²ⁿ, which is refuted at every p.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Four shapes: which of these is not context-free; is this grammar’s pumping length valid; what does the split force; which statement is false.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
The procedure: count the independent equalities. One stack tracks one, so two at once across three blocks is the usual answer.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Then check the direction twice: surviving the lemma proves nothing, and a failed small p proves nothing either.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
One line to carry: the lemma kills languages, never saves them.

